## Acceptance

To get any licence under these terms, you must agree to them as both strict
obligations and conditions to all your licences.

## Copyright licence

The licensor grants you a copyright licence to do everything with the software that
would otherwise infringe the licensor's copyright in it, for any permitted purpose,
other than distributing the software, making changes or new works based on the
software, or operating it as a competing service.

## Any purpose is a permitted purpose

Commercial use, personal use, research, teaching and evaluation are all permitted
purposes. Nothing in these terms limits what you may use the software *for*. The
limits are on copying it, on changing it and on redeploying it as your own service.

## Reading it and running it yourself

Reading, auditing, running, instrumenting, load testing and benchmarking the software
is permitted, including deploying your own instance to check that it does what it
says. Publishing what you learn by doing so is permitted. Facts about how the software
behaves are yours.

Calling the licensor's own deployed endpoints is not restricted by this licence at
all. They are a public service and this licence is about the source.

## Keeping a copy to verify

You may download and keep copies for as long as you need them to audit the code,
reproduce a measurement or archive what you reviewed.

## What is not granted

These terms grant no permission to:

1. distribute the software, in whole or in part, with or without a fee, whether by
   hosting it, mirroring it, committing it to a repository or serving it from a URL;

2. make changes or new works based on the software. This includes, and is not limited
   to, a copy with a handler, route, parser or provider added, removed or renamed; a
   copy with a threshold, alias table or answer template changed; and a copy that
   keeps the answer format while replacing the data source behind it;

3. operate the software, or anything based on it, as a service that competes with the
   licensor's own deployment of it. Registering it, or a modification of it, as your
   own miner, endpoint, agent or entry in any registry, protocol, competition or
   marketplace is not permitted;

4. present the software, or anything based on it, as your own work or under an identity
   other than the licensor's.

Points 2 and 3 are spelled out because the way this software gets taken is not a
source fork. It is a redeploy: copy the worker, point it at the same free API, keep
the answer shape that a scoring module rewards, and register it as a separate entry.
That is the act these terms withhold.

## Forking on GitHub

This repository is public on GitHub, and GitHub's terms of service grant every GitHub
user a licence to view and fork public repositories through GitHub's own
functionality. That grant sits above this licence and these terms do not try to
override it. Forking the repository on GitHub is fine.

Note what that grant covers, in GitHub's own words: to "use, display, perform and
reproduce (by forking)". It does not grant the right to make changes or new works, and
GitHub says broader permissions have to come from the repository's own licence. This
licence does not give them. So a fork is a copy you may keep. It is not permission to
deploy a changed copy as your own service.

## Third-party data

The data this software serves is not the licensor's and is not covered by these terms.
It belongs to the upstream providers named in `NOTICE`, under their own licences and
their own limits, some of which restrict commercial use and redistribution. `NOTICE`
and `DATA-SOURCES.md` record what each one requires. Running your own instance means
you deal with those providers directly and their terms bind you, not us.

## Fair use

You may have fair use or fair dealing rights under the law. These terms do not limit
them.

## No other rights

These terms do not allow you to sublicense or transfer any of your licences to anyone
else, and do not prevent the licensor from granting licences to anyone else. They imply
no other licences.

## Patent licence

The licensor grants you a patent licence for the software that covers patent claims the
licensor can license, or becomes able to license, that you would infringe by using the
software.

## Patent defence

If you make any written claim that the software infringes or contributes to
infringement of any patent, your patent licence for the software ends immediately. If
your company makes such a claim, your patent licence ends immediately for work on
behalf of your company.

## Violations

The first time you are notified in writing that you have violated these terms, or done
something with the software not covered by your licences, your licences can continue if
you come into full compliance and take practical steps to correct past violations
within 32 days of receiving that notice. Otherwise all your licences end immediately.

## No liability

***As far as the law allows, the software comes as is, without any warranty or
condition, and the licensor will not be liable to you for any damages arising out of
these terms or the use or nature of the software, under any kind of legal claim.***

## Definitions

The **licensor** is zkasuran. The **software** is the files in this repository that the
licensor authored and makes available under these terms.

**You** refers to the individual or entity agreeing to these terms.

**Your company** is any legal entity, sole proprietorship or other organisation you
work for, plus every organisation that controls it, that it controls, or that is under
common control with it. **Control** means ownership of substantially all the assets of
an entity, or the power to direct its management and policies by vote, contract or
otherwise. Control can be direct or indirect.

**Your licences** are all the licences granted to you for the software under these
terms.

**Use** means anything you do with the software requiring one of your licences.

## Drafting note

The structure and much of the plain-language wording of this licence is adapted from a
standardised source-available licence template, with its noncommercial restriction
replaced by the "any purpose" grant above and its identifying references removed, as
that template's own terms require of an adaptation.
